<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    SYSTEM DPI awareness, deliberately not PerMonitorV2.

    Per-monitor awareness makes Windows send WM_DPICHANGED as the window crosses to a screen with
    different scaling, and Slint mishandles the rescale: the window ends up the wrong size and the
    content is drawn at the wrong scale inside it. That is slint-ui/slint#11073 (open, marked
    upstream) and rust-windowing/winit#3040.

    Two attempts to keep per-monitor awareness and correct for the bug both failed the same way,
    and both are worth remembering so they are not tried a third time:
      - v1.0.4 swallowed WM_DPICHANGED. The frame changed while the renderer stayed at the old
        scale, so the content was drawn at the wrong size inside the window.
      - v1.0.6 let the message through and then forced the window to the rect Windows supplies.
        That corrects the frame but not Slint's internal size, so the layout desynced exactly as
        in v1.0.4: content offset and clipped past the right edge.
    The lesson is that the frame and the renderer have to be corrected together, through Slint's
    own API, not behind its back at the Win32 level.

    With system awareness the window is never asked to rescale. It keeps one size and Windows
    composites it for the other screen, so dragging between monitors is stable. The cost is that
    it looks slightly soft on a monitor whose scaling differs from the system setting, which is a
    far better trade than the window resizing and rendering incorrectly.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
